## sess130 — RULE-5 ruling on blockers 2+3; state landed on 0.11.446

Tree `0.11.445` → **0.11.446**, srcversion `9A4FD08BE505182F28316CD`, builds clean. Not deployed; fleet stays on 0.11.440. **Still STOP-SHIP.**

I continued D-SAMENODE-WAITER-CANCEL-COLLISION (ledger #16) rather than starting at #1 — same reason as sess119–129: the DLM core is mid-landing under stacked stop-ship rulings and sess126 set the order.

**The consult earned its cost — my design was wrong.** I proposed gating admission at `lreq_join`, waiting for in-flight to hit zero, and leaving `release_all` where it is (before the drain). GPT found the race that makes the ordering mandatory, and it exists in the shipping code today: `release_all` clears every holder bit → an acquire *already inside the door* completes, re-takes a bit, and publishes nothing because it **succeeded** → the quiesce sees it finish, the drain finds no obligation, and the node broadcasts GOODBYE while still holding the bit on disk. `release_all` has to run *after* the gate closes and the count reaches zero, in an exclusive phase.

Four more rulings that reshape the design: a quiesce **timeout must not permit teardown** (proceeding destroys the exact claim being proved); GOODBYE and the clean slot release belong only on the clean-success branch; escalation needs **two** triggers, since the 2s drain deadline and the runtime age deadline are different clocks; and the callback must not be the safety latch — the DLM records failed/closed/no-clean-departure synchronously under the lock *before* invoking it.

**Three code facts worth keeping.** The schedule-after-cancel race GPT flagged closes itself: `put_super` NULLs `m_mxfs_dlm` before `cancel_work_sync`, and `mxfs_dlm_shutdown_withdraw` early-returns on that NULL, so a shutdown-path escalation can't re-arm the work — which is why the recorded-state design is load-bearing rather than belt-and-braces. `mxfs_dlm_caw_destroy` calls `release_all` *after* `mxfs_dlm_caw_stop`, so anything it publishes can never be collected — and it runs unconditionally, defeating the `withdrawn` D2 freeze that `mxfs_v5_dlm_shutdown` carefully gates. And `MXFS_CAW_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS` is 120000, so any quiesce deadline under ~120s makes escalation routine rather than exceptional.

**Landed:** the ctx admission-gate state (`ops_closed`/`ops_active`/`ops_refused`), `departed_clean` (fail-closed at false), the escalation latch fields, and the episode clock — `owed_since_ms` stamped only on the not-pending→pending edge, cleared where the per-episode reset already lives. All inert until the lifecycle restructure wires it up, so 0.11.446 is behaviourally identical to 0.11.445. The five remaining steps are recorded in ccmemory `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess130-GPT-ruling-blockers-2-3-lifecycle` in the order they have to land.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical, DLM core under two stacked stop-ship rulings with blockers 2 and 3 only partially landed. Marker unwritten.

